    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a debilitating neurodegenerative disease currently afflicting millions for which no disease-modifying treatments exist. Neuroinflammation has been heavily implicated in AD progression and is a potential therapeutic target. In addition to the neurotoxicity caused by amyloid beta (Aβ) and tau, microglial activity causes secondary toxicity and may exacerbate Aβ and tau pathologies. Triggering receptor expressed on myeloid cells 2 (TREM2) promotes phagocytosis and has been identified as a risk variant associated with AD using genetic approaches, but its role is not fully understood. TREM1 signals via the same pathway, and it has been identified as a potent amplifier of toxic immune responses in acute inflammatory settings but remains relatively unexplored in AD. Using immunohistochemistry, we examined microglial activity and TREM1 and TREM2 expression in mouse brain tissue with intracerebroventricular Aβ delivery. We measured microglial activity by examining process length, number of processes, and CD68, a phagocytosis marker. We also developed and verified a monocyte-derived, microglia-like cell (MDMi) model to provide more direct evidence of the microglial response to Aβ. We carried out an Aβ-dosing study where we examined the response of TREM1 and TREM2 in MDMi cells with exposure to escalating levels of Aβ. We additionally developed an IHC protocol to examine human brain tissue from AD patients and confirmed TREM1 expression. Our results support increased microglial activity upon exposure to Aβ, as well as an increase in TREM1 and decrease in TREM2 with Aβ. These data have led us to propose a model whereby TREM1 activity promotes the maladaptive immune response by decreasing expression of protective TREM2. With further exploration, the regulatory roles of TREM1 and TREM2 in development of pathologic inflammation could be fully revealed and hold clinical and therapeutic relevance.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
